Obviously, neither I nor any small group could technically carry that
out. Restoring backups is too easy. Besides, it would be vandalism
under their terms of use. I'm not even sure if a deletion spree would
achieve its goal at all -- Google would see the activity and
counteractivity, and conclude the old site is live and kicking.

In my opinion, all that energy and anger can be used more productively
on the new site because Google sends visitors to the most active site.
Plenty of things need work.There is some bad advice on the wiki. There
is a lot more advice that makes assumptions on the player-skill level,
and becomes mis-advice for everybody else. From a marketing point of
view, it might be worthwhile to structure/split pages so that each
section is well-aimed at a different segment of the player community.
Skill level and conducts are obvious things to segment by, but also
the mental effort readers want to invest. I believe the Rodney
database is so popular because it caters to people who want a short,
easy, and mostly correct explanation. For the wiki this could mean
that every page should get a paragraph aimed at people in a hurry, and
only after that should it delve into corner cases, precise mechanics,
and so on.

That was just off the top of my head. Perhaps a cluster analysis on
Patric's data and some market research what people would like to read
could help.


